[DOCUMENT]
TITLE: Prose for description, lists for anything the reader must unpack

[GRAMMAR]
IMPORT_FROM_FILE: @repo

[INVARIANT]
UID: INV-LISTS-WHERE-THE-READER-UNPACKS
TITLE: Prose for description, lists for anything the reader must unpack
DEPTH: interface-settled
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
Descriptive content may be paragraphs. Anything the reader must MENTALLY UNPACK
-- options, steps, findings, obligations, open questions -- is a list.

Brief and informative beats complete and long. A wall of text is a defect in its
own right, independent of whether every sentence in it is true, because the
reader cannot find the one sentence that needed them.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
A list makes the count of things visible before any of them is read, which is
what lets a reader decide what to unpack and what to skip. Prose hides the
count. The rule is about the reader's attention, not about style.
<<<
NOTES: >>>
Recorded 2026-08-28 at the operator's request, as one of four contributor-communication requirements (INV-DECISIONS-ARRIVE-AS-CARDS, INV-LISTS-WHERE-THE-READER-UNPACKS, INV-FILE-DO-NOT-NARRATE, INV-SETTLED-STAYS-VISIBLE). The rule is the operator's; AUTHORED_BY reads llm because the writer has no flag for the other value. An INVARIANT is read as a REQUIREMENT under DEC-INVARIANT-IS-A-REQUIREMENT; the grammar type is kept so the grid is not half-applied. The sdoc skill's backlog protocol is the procedure these four are the requirement for.
<<<
